    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d960">

                <pb facs="0912" n="598" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg598"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/543</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Chargé d’Affaires</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Barclay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-12">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 12, 1914</hi>.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">No. 264]</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my notes Nos. 252<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d960-f1"/> and 259 of August 4 and August 9, respectively,
                    stating and explaining the position taken up by His Majesty’s Government in
                    regard to the question of armed merchantmen, I have the honour to state that I
                    have now been informed by Sir Edward Grey
                    that exactly similar instructions were at the same time issued by him to His
                    Majesty’s representatives in practically all neutral countries to address the
                    same communications to the respective Governments to which they were
                    accredited.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Colville Barclay</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
